# Words 301-350 (phrasal verbs PHR-051..100)
R = [
("PHR-051","put off – put off – put off – putting off","'Put off' is followed by -ing, not 'to + verb'.","PUT OFF: push a job OFF to later, like a snooze button.","'Put off' also means make someone dislike something: 'The smell put me off.'","Write a sentence about a task you keep putting off.",("see a boring task","decide to do it later","it's still not done"),"put off vs cancel","Put off = delay to a later time. Cancel = decide it won't happen at all."),
("PHR-052","set up – set up – set up – setting up","'Set' has the same form in the past; 'setted' is wrong.","SET → SET → SET, like PUT → PUT → PUT.","'Set up' also means trick someone: 'He was set up.' A 'setup' (noun) is an arrangement.","Write a sentence about something you set up at home or work.",("have an idea","arrange everything needed","it's ready to run"),"set up vs start","Start = begin. Set up = arrange everything needed so something can start (set up a business)."),
("PHR-053","take over – took over – taken over – taking over","'Last week' needs the past 'took', and 'from' isn't needed before a time.","TAKE OVER: take the steering wheel OVER from someone.","'Takeover' (noun) = one company buying control of another.","Write a sentence about a job you took over from someone.",("someone leaves a role","step in","now you control it"),"take over vs take on","Take over = get control from someone else. Take on = accept new work or responsibility."),
("PHR-054","take on – took on – taken on – taking on","'Take on' takes its object directly, with no 'with'.","TAKE ON: put a new load ON your shoulders.","'Take on' also means compete against: 'India will take on Australia.'","Write a sentence about a challenge you took on this year.",("see a new task","agree to do it","it's now your responsibility"),"take on vs take over","Take on = accept extra work. Take over = take control from someone else."),
("PHR-055","hand in – handed in – handed in – handing in","The thing comes after 'hand in'; the person comes after 'to'.","HAND IN: your HAND puts work IN the teacher's tray.","Schools: 'hand in homework'. Offices: 'hand in your resignation'.","Write a sentence about when you have to hand in your next assignment.",("finish your homework","give it to the teacher","it's submitted"),"hand in vs submit","Same meaning. Submit is more formal and used online.","I handed in the teacher my homework.","I handed in my homework to the teacher."),
("PHR-056","hand out – handed out – handed out – handing out","Put the thing right after 'hand out', then 'to' + the people.","HAND OUT: hands go OUT to each person in a row.","A 'handout' (noun) = a printed sheet given in class.","Write a sentence about something that was handed out in your class.",("have a stack of sheets","give one to each person","everyone has one"),"hand out vs hand in","Hand out = give to many people. Hand in = give your work to one person in charge."),
("PHR-057","hand over – handed over – handed over – handing over","'Hand over' is followed directly by 'to' + person, with no 'for'.","HAND OVER: pass something OVER the table to someone else.","'Handover' (noun) = the formal passing of work from one person to another.","Write a sentence about something you handed over before leaving a job or class.",("hold the keys","give them to someone formally","they are responsible now"),"hand over vs give","Give is general. Hand over = give control or ownership, often formally."),
("PHR-058","follow up – followed up – followed up – following up","Use 'follow up WITH' a person and 'follow up ON' a matter.","FOLLOW UP: follow the first step UP with a second one.","'Just following up on my last email' is a standard work email line.","Write an email line following up on a job application.",("send an email","get no reply","write again to check"),"follow up vs follow","Follow = go after or obey. Follow up = take the next action after a first step."),
("PHR-059","follow through – followed through – followed through – following through","Use 'follow through ON' something.","FOLLOW THROUGH: go all the way THROUGH to the end.","In sport, 'follow-through' is completing the swing of a bat or racket.","Write a sentence about a promise you followed through on.",("make a promise","keep working at it","finish it completely"),"follow through vs follow up","Follow up = take the next step. Follow through = finish what you started.","He never follows through his promises.","He never follows through on his promises."),
("PHR-060","back up – backed up – backed up – backing up","'Back up' takes its object directly, with no 'with'.","BACK UP: keep a copy at the BACK in case the front fails.","'Back me up' = support what I say. 'Backup' (noun) = a copy.","Write a sentence about how often you back up your phone photos.",("have important files","copy them to another place","they're safe if the phone breaks"),"back up vs support","Support = help in general. Back up = support someone's words or claim; or copy data."),
("PHR-061","break down – broke down – broken down – breaking down","'Break' is irregular: the past is 'broke', not 'broked'.","BREAK DOWN: the machine breaks and falls DOWN.","'Break down' also means cry, or explain step by step: 'break down the problem'.","Write a sentence about a time a vehicle broke down.",("the car is running","the engine stops suddenly","it won't move"),"break down vs break up","Break down = stop working, or cry. Break up = end a relationship."),
("PHR-062","cut down – cut down – cut down – cutting down","Use 'cut down ON' + the thing you are reducing.","CUT DOWN: cut the amount DOWN.","'Cut down' also means cut a tree to the ground.","Write a sentence about something you want to cut down on.",("use too much sugar","use less each week","use much less"),"cut down on vs cut out","Cut down on = reduce. Cut out = stop completely."),
("PHR-063","cut off – cut off – cut off – cutting off","A sudden finished event uses the simple past.","CUT OFF: like scissors cutting a line.","'I got cut off' = my phone call ended suddenly.","Write a sentence about a time your call got cut off.",("talk on the phone","the signal drops","the call ends"),"cut off vs cut out","Cut off = stop suddenly (a call, supply). Cut out = remove, or stop eating something."),
("PHR-064","check in – checked in – checked in – checking in","Use 'check in AT' the place, or 'check in to/into' a hotel.","CHECK IN: your name goes IN the hotel's list.","'Check in' also means contact someone to see how they are.","Write a sentence about when you checked in at an airport or hotel.",("arrive at the hotel","show ID at reception","get your room key"),"check in vs check out","Check in = register when you arrive. Check out = pay and leave."),
("PHR-065","check out – checked out – checked out – checking out","Use 'check out OF' a hotel, not 'from', and the past tense 'checked'.","CHECK OUT: your name comes OUT of the hotel's list.","Informal: 'Check this out!' = look at this.","Write a sentence about something online you want to check out.",("finish your stay","pay the bill","leave the hotel"),"check out vs leave","Leave = go away. Check out = formally finish your stay and pay."),
("PHR-066","call off – called off – called off – calling off","'Call off' takes its object directly; no 'of' after 'off'.","CALL OFF: call out 'It's OFF!'.","'The match was called off due to rain.'","Write a sentence about an event that was called off.",("plan a trip","something goes wrong","announce it won't happen"),"call off vs postpone","Call off = cancel. Postpone = move to a later date."),
("PHR-067","call back – called back – called back – calling back","The person goes between 'call' and 'back', with no 'to'.","CALL BACK: the call comes BACK to the person who called first.","'Can I call you back?' is polite when you're busy.","Write a sentence promising to call someone back.",("miss a call","see the number","phone them"),"call back vs call again","Call back = return a call someone made to you. Call again = phone once more."),
("PHR-068","put up with – put up with – put up with – putting up with","'Put up with' is followed by a noun or -ing, not 'to + verb'.","PUT UP WITH: carry a heavy load UP the hill WITH patience.","It shows annoyance: 'I can't put up with this noise any more.'","Write a sentence about something you have to put up with daily.",("something annoys you","stay patient","accept it without complaining"),"put up with vs tolerate","Same meaning. Tolerate is more formal."),
("PHR-069","run out – ran out – run out – running out","Use the past 'ran', and 'run out OF' + the thing.","RUN OUT: the supply RUNS until it's OUT.","'Time is running out' = there's little time left.","Write a sentence about something that ran out at home this week.",("have some milk","use it all","none is left"),"run out vs finish","Finish = complete something on purpose. Run out (of) = have nothing left, often by surprise."),
("PHR-070","run into – ran into – run into – running into","'Yesterday' needs the past 'ran'.","RUN INTO: run around a corner and INTO a friend.","'Run into problems' = meet unexpected problems.","Write a sentence about someone you ran into by chance.",("walk in the mall","see a familiar face","stop and chat"),"run into vs bump into","Same meaning for people. 'Run into' is also used for problems and costs."),
("PHR-071","get through – got through – got/gotten through – getting through","Use 'get through TO' a person on the phone.","GET THROUGH: go THROUGH a difficult tunnel to the other end.","Three meanings: reach by phone, finish something, survive a hard time.","Write a sentence about how you got through a hard exam.",("face a hard time","keep going","reach the other side"),"get through vs pass","Pass = succeed in an exam. Get through = finish or survive something difficult."),
("PHR-072","get across – got across – got/gotten across – getting across","'Team' needs 'the'. With a noun object, 'get the idea across' is most natural.","GET ACROSS: carry your idea ACROSS a bridge to the listener.","'I'm trying to get my point across' is common in debates.","Write a sentence about how you get an idea across in a meeting.",("have an idea","explain it clearly","the listener understands"),"get across vs explain","Explain = say how or why. Get across = succeed in making someone understand."),
("PHR-073","go through – went through – gone through – going through","'A lot' needs 'of' before a noun: 'a lot of problems'.","GO THROUGH: walk THROUGH a difficult forest.","'Go through the document' = read it carefully.","Write a sentence about a difficult time someone you know went through.",("a hard time starts","live through it","it ends"),"go through vs go over","Go through = experience, or examine in detail. Go over = review quickly."),
("PHR-074","go over – went over – gone over – going over","'Go over' takes its object directly, with no 'on'.","GO OVER: your eyes go OVER the page again.","'Let's go over it once more' is common before exams.","Write a sentence about what you go over before a test.",("finish a report","read it once more","fix any mistakes"),"go over vs go through","Go over = review or check. Go through = examine every detail, or experience something hard."),
("PHR-075","go ahead – went ahead – gone ahead – going ahead","Before a second verb, use 'go ahead AND + verb', not 'to'.","GO AHEAD: move AHEAD — the traffic light is green.","'Go ahead' alone is a polite 'yes, please do'.","Write a sentence giving someone permission with 'Go ahead'.",("someone asks permission","say 'Go ahead'","they start"),"go ahead vs continue","Go ahead = start, with permission. Continue = keep doing something already started."),
("PHR-076","go on – went on – gone on – going on","'Go on + -ing' = continue. The past is 'went'.","GO ON: the show goes ON.","'What's going on?' = what's happening? 'Go on!' = encouragement.","Write a sentence asking a friend what's going on.",("someone starts talking","they don't stop","they talk for an hour"),"go on doing vs go on to do","Go on talking = continue the same thing. Go on to talk about X = move to a new topic next."),
("PHR-077","keep up – kept up – kept up – keeping up","Use 'keep up WITH' + what you're matching.","KEEP UP: keep UP with the runner beside you.","'Keep it up!' = continue doing well.","Write a sentence about a subject you find hard to keep up with.",("the class moves fast","work hard","stay at the same level"),"keep up with vs catch up with","Keep up = stay level. Catch up = reach the level after falling behind."),
("PHR-078","keep on – kept on – kept on – keeping on","'Keep on' takes -ing, and he/she needs -s: keeps.","KEEP ON: keep ON going, like a switch that stays on.","'Keep on trying' is encouragement.","Write a sentence about something someone keeps on doing that annoys you.",("start an action","repeat it","still doing it"),"keep on vs keep","Same meaning with -ing. Keep on often suggests the action is annoying or determined."),
("PHR-079","catch on – caught on – caught on – catching on","'Catch' is irregular (caught), and use 'catch on TO' something.","CATCH ON: an idea catches ON like fire.","'Catch on' also means become popular: 'The trend caught on fast.'","Write a sentence about a trend that caught on in your city.",("hear a joke","think for a moment","finally understand it"),"catch on vs understand","Understand is general. Catch on = understand after a delay, or become popular."),
("PHR-080","pick up – picked up – picked up – picking up","With a pronoun, it goes in the middle: 'pick you up'.","PICK UP: your hand picks something UP.","Many meanings: collect someone, learn casually ('pick up Hindi'), answer a phone.","Write a sentence about who picks you up after school or work.",("wait at the station","a car arrives","get in"),"pick up vs collect","Same meaning for people and things. Collect is slightly more formal."),
("PHR-081","put away – put away – put away – putting away","'Put away' needs no 'to'.","PUT AWAY: put it AWAY from sight, in its place.","'Put away money' = save it.","Write a sentence about what you put away after dinner.",("finish using things","take them to their places","the room is tidy"),"put away vs put back","Put away = store in its proper place. Put back = return to exactly where it was."),
("PHR-082","put back – put back – put back – putting back","With a pronoun like 'it', it goes between 'put' and 'back'.","PUT BACK: return it BACK to its spot.","'Put back' also means delay: 'The meeting was put back a week.'","Write a sentence about something you forgot to put back.",("take a book from the shelf","use it","return it to the same spot"),"put back vs put away","Put back = return to where it was. Put away = store tidily."),
("PHR-083","put down – put down – put down – putting down","A reflexive pronoun goes between 'put' and 'down': 'put himself down'.","PUT DOWN: move something DOWN onto a surface — or push a person's confidence down.","'Put down' also means kill a sick animal kindly (vet).","Write a sentence about why we shouldn't put others down.",("hold a heavy bag","lower it","it rests on the floor"),"put down vs put off","Put down = place on a surface, or criticise. Put off = delay."),
("PHR-084","take back – took back – taken back – taking back","'Take back' already means withdraw your words, so 'to you' isn't needed.","TAKE BACK: pull your words BACK into your mouth.","Shops: 'Can I take this back?' = return it.","Write a sentence about something you said that you wanted to take back.",("say something unkind","feel sorry","say 'I take it back'"),"take back vs return","Return = give back or go back. Take back = return an item to a shop, or withdraw words."),
("PHR-085","take off – took off – taken off – taking off","'Yesterday' needs the past 'took'.","TAKE OFF: the plane lifts OFF the ground.","'Take off' also means remove clothes, or a sudden success: 'Her career took off.'","Write a sentence about what you take off when you get home.",("the plane runs down the runway","its wheels leave the ground","it climbs into the sky"),"take off vs put on","Opposites for clothes: put on = wear; take off = remove."),
("PHR-086","take away – took away – taken away – taking away","'Before I finished' shows the past, so use 'took'.","TAKE AWAY: take it AWAY from here.","In Indian and British English, 'takeaway' = food you carry home.","Write a sentence about something a teacher took away from a student.",("the plate is on the table","the waiter picks it up","the table is clear"),"take away vs remove","Same meaning. Take away is everyday; remove is more formal."),
("PHR-087","throw away – threw away – thrown away – throwing away","'Throw' is irregular: the past is 'threw'.","THROW AWAY: throw it far AWAY into the bin.","'Throw away a chance' = waste an opportunity.","Write a sentence about something you threw away recently.",("find old shoes","put them in the bin","they're gone"),"throw away vs throw out","Same meaning for rubbish. Throw out also means force someone to leave."),
("PHR-088","throw out – threw out – thrown out – throwing out","Use 'threw', and the pronoun goes in the middle: 'threw him out OF'.","THROW OUT: throw someone OUT of the door.","'Throw out an idea' = reject it.","Write a sentence about a rule for when someone gets thrown out of a class.",("someone behaves badly","the manager asks them to leave","they're outside"),"throw out vs throw away","Both mean discard things. Only throw out is used for forcing a person to leave."),
("PHR-089","give back – gave back – given back – giving back","With a pronoun person, put it after 'give': 'give me back my keys'.","GIVE BACK: give it BACK to where it came from.","'Give back to society' = help your community.","Write a sentence about something you borrowed and gave back.",("borrow a book","finish it","return it to the owner"),"give back vs return","Same meaning. Return is more formal."),
("PHR-090","give away – gave away – given away – giving away","Put the object before 'away', then 'to + person'.","GIVE AWAY: give it AWAY for free.","'Giveaway' (noun) = a free gift or contest. 'Give away a secret' = reveal it.","Write a sentence about something you gave away to someone in need.",("have old clothes","give them free to others","they're gone"),"give away vs give up","Give away = give for free, or reveal. Give up = stop trying."),
("PHR-091","give in – gave in – given in – giving in","Use 'give in TO' + the pressure or person.","GIVE IN: let the other side come IN — you stop resisting.","Parents often 'give in' to children who cry for sweets.","Write a sentence about a time you gave in to a temptation.",("refuse again and again","feel pressure","finally agree"),"give in vs give up","Give in = stop resisting and agree. Give up = stop trying."),
("PHR-092","give out – gave out – given out – giving out","Both verbs need the past: 'gave' and 'started'.","GIVE OUT: hand things OUT to many people.","'My legs gave out' = they stopped working from tiredness.","Write a sentence about who gives out prizes at your school.",("hold a box of prizes","hand one to each winner","everyone has a prize"),"give out vs hand out","Same meaning for distributing. Give out also means stop working (a machine or body)."),
("PHR-093","pass away – passed away – passed away – passing away","With a finished past time, use 'ago', not 'since'.","PASS AWAY: gently PASS from this world.","It's the polite, respectful way to say 'die'.","Write a respectful sentence using 'passed away'.",("someone is very ill","their life ends peacefully","family remembers them"),"pass away vs die","Same meaning. Pass away is gentler and more polite."),
("PHR-094","pass out – passed out – passed out – passing out","Keep both parts of the sentence in the past: 'was tired'.","PASS OUT: your mind goes OUT for a moment.","'Pass out' also means graduate (Indian English: 'passed out of college in 2020').","Write a sentence about what to do if someone passes out.",("feel dizzy","fall to the floor","wake up after a moment"),"pass out vs faint","Same meaning. Faint is more formal."),
("PHR-095","pass on – passed on – passed on – passing on","Put the object before 'on', then 'to + person'.","PASS ON: pass the ball ON to the next player.","'Pass on a message' = tell it to someone else.","Write a sentence asking someone to pass on a message.",("get a message","tell the next person","they know it too"),"pass on vs pass away","Pass on = give to someone else. 'Pass on' can also politely mean die, like 'pass away'."),
("PHR-096","pay off – paid off – paid off – paying off","After a verb, use the adverb 'well', not the adjective 'good'.","PAY OFF: the effort PAYS you back in the end.","'Pay off a loan' = repay it completely.","Write a sentence about hard work that paid off for you.",("work hard for months","see results slowly","succeed"),"pay off vs pay back","Pay off = repay a whole debt, or be worth it. Pay back = return borrowed money."),
("PHR-097","pay back – paid back – paid back – paying back","No 'to': put the person between 'pay' and 'back'.","PAY BACK: the money goes BACK to where it came from.","'Payback' (noun) informally means revenge.","Write a sentence about when you will pay back a loan.",("borrow money","save it up","return it"),"pay back vs pay off","Pay back = return money to a person. Pay off = clear a debt completely."),
("PHR-098","turn down – turned down – turned down – turning down","A finished past action needs 'turned'.","TURN DOWN: turn your thumb DOWN = no.","'Turn down' also means lower volume: 'Turn down the music.'","Write a sentence about an offer you turned down.",("receive an offer","think about it","say no politely"),"turn down vs refuse","Same meaning. Turn down is softer and common for offers and invitations."),
("PHR-099","turn off – turned off – turned off – turning off","With a pronoun like 'it', it goes between 'turn' and 'off'.","TURN OFF: the switch goes to OFF.","'That's a turn-off' = something that makes you lose interest.","Write a sentence about what you turn off before sleeping.",("the light is on","press the switch","the room is dark"),"turn off vs switch off","Same meaning. Switch off is more common in Indian and British English."),
("PHR-100","turn on – turned on – turned on – turning on","With a pronoun like 'it', it goes between 'turn' and 'on'.","TURN ON: the switch goes to ON.","Careful: 'turned on' can also mean sexually excited in informal English.","Write a sentence about the first thing you turn on in the morning.",("the room is dark","press the switch","the light comes on"),"turn on vs switch on","Same meaning. Switch on is more common in Indian and British English."),
]
